WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.100
SOUVIK CHOUDHURY: So
the topic for today

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:12.840
is how to migrate windows
workloads to Google Cloud.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:16.620
And this foundationally
gets back

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:20.730
to Google's principle of meeting
our customers where they are

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:24.030
and helping them migrate
to Google Cloud and a more

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:26.820
open collaborative
architecture for applications

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:31.020
at their own pace,
and in a model that

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:32.680
is more comfortable for them.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.640
Some of that is around
rebuilding your applications,

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.490
but a lot of that is also
around lifting and shifting

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:42.060
of your enterprise applications
onto GCP or writing new .NET

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:42.720
Applications.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.960
And that's the
strategy behind making

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.430
sure Windows is a Tier I
platform, a first class

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:53.690
platform for running
Windows workloads on GCP.

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:56.300
We have been at it
for a few years now.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:02.360
We started in 2015 where we
launched our first Windows

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.819
Server offerings on GCP.

00:01:04.819 --> 00:01:11.690
And since then, we have been
pretty hard at work adding

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:13.870
additional capabilities,
like SQL Server support,

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:16.400
Azure support, various
types of clustering,

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:18.980
and failover support, as
well as toolings around .NET

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:23.970
and PowerShell for making
GCP a great platform for us.

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:26.900
And today, my colleague Simon--

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:27.783
and I'm Souvik.

00:01:27.783 --> 00:01:29.450
I'm part of the product
management team,

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:31.790
and Simon is part of
the engineering team.

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:34.520
We are going to spend a
bit of time kind of walking

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:39.740
through the migration journey
for Windows workloads to GCP

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:44.780
and announce a few new products
and offerings that I believe

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:49.390
will be super, super interesting
to many of you in the room.

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:52.810
The first of all, I
just wanted to thank you

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:56.200
for literally tens of thousands
of customers who are running

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.270
Windows workflows on GCP.

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:00.498
That may come as a
surprise to a lot of you,

00:02:00.498 --> 00:02:02.290
because we have been
kind of creeping along

00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:05.650
with ad features, getting
customers along the way.

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:09.880
I can happily say that
the number of customers

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:12.760
who is running Windows
workloads on GCP

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:14.390
is actually really significant.

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:17.260
So if any of you are kind
of hesitant and not sure

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:20.980
if you are going to take the
first step, because there

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:24.700
is not too many customers
on GCP on Windows,

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:27.640
that should definitely
not be a concern anymore.

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:31.120
And the conversation here
more about how to get there

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:33.190
and the details of the
navigation process,

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:36.180
more so than if.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:38.740
So when we go out and talk
to these tens of thousands

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:42.380
of customers, a representative
of them, like many of you here,

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:45.421
why are they running
Windows on GCP,

00:02:45.421 --> 00:02:49.760
a couple of points come
out loud and clear.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:52.780
So the number one point that
comes out loud and clear

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:57.250
is Google's position
on being open.

00:02:57.250 --> 00:02:59.710
We are not a vendor
who wants to lock you

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:02.800
into proprietary technology
or proprietary platform.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:03.970
We are open.

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:06.550
And I think a lot of
you have this vision

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:10.520
of running your applications
in an open and dynamic cloud,

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:14.320
where you can migrate across
various public cloud platforms

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:16.110
as well as on-premises.

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:17.610
And that's some of
the announcements

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:19.510
that have been made this week.

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:21.250
A second is that
Google did everything.

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:24.830
We have amazing infrastructure,
network security, efficiency,

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:26.000
et cetera, et cetera.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.750
And all of those capabilities
apply to Windows workloads

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:29.390
as well.

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:31.270
We are not going to
touch on them too much,

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:32.590
because this is a pretty big--

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:34.190
Windows is a pretty big topic.

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:36.760
But those play a big role.

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:39.340
The third thing that
we hear is that Windows

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:45.220
is a big part of any enterprise,
data center, or workload.

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:50.500
So as customers move, as you
move many of your workloads,

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:52.210
the Windows
dependency components

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:53.680
need to be supported well.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:57.700
And that makes up a significant
percentage of the workloads.

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:00.730
And the last one that comes
across pretty frequently also

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:03.400
is the data gravity
and integration

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:05.020
with Google applications.

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:08.470
If many of our customers have
very significant amount of data

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:11.620
on GCP as well as they're
building newer applications

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:15.730
with .NET, .NET Core, and
other languages on GCP,

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:18.070
and if there is a Windows
platform dependency,

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:20.860
running them on GCP just makes
it much more easier, simpler,

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:24.740
secure than running it outside.

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:27.080
So the question then
becomes, how do we get there?

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:28.550
What is the journey?

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:32.500
And having kind of observed this
across many, many customers,

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:34.000
this is kind of
the simple pattern

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.950
that we see again and again.

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:37.930
The journey starts
with, hey, I really

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:39.360
don't know what to start.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.110
And they go through
an assessment planning

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:42.520
and a migration phase.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:45.160
Then they kind of
think about how

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:48.520
do you operate large complex
Windows environments on GCP?

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:51.670
And then they simultaneously
with that or subsequent

00:04:51.670 --> 00:04:53.740
to that there's a
phase of, how do

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:56.830
we actually modernize our
Windows applications on GCP?

00:04:56.830 --> 00:05:00.930
And we're going to touch on
all of these topics today.

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:03.510
And it's a pretty broad
span of activities.

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:05.260
And we want actually
to demo a few things.

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:06.820
So we would not
necessarily have time

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:09.550
to get into the details of
many of these components.

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:11.980
But there are a number
of individual sessions

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:15.670
that have already happened or
happening in the next two days

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:19.210
to talk about these in details.

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:21.240
So these are some
of the key questions

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:23.400
that we hear from
our customers as they

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:26.970
get started on migrating
their Windows components

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:29.520
or applications to GCP.

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:32.520
The first one is,
how do I start?

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:35.430
The next big thing that
is very Windows specific

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:39.280
is, how does the
licensing thing play out?

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:42.990
Do I have to rebuy all my
Microsoft licenses on GCP?

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:45.180
And lastly, what
exact technologies do

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:47.370
we use for migrating the VMs?

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:50.435
And how do I test and make sure
everything is working together?

00:05:50.435 --> 00:05:54.360
And let's touch on
those questions.

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:55.970
But before getting
into the details,

00:05:55.970 --> 00:05:57.860
and just given the scope
of the information,

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:00.620
I just wanted to highlight
this migration center website,

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:03.470
which is at
cloud.google.com/migrate.

00:06:03.470 --> 00:06:06.560
It's the single place for
accessing all the information

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:08.300
regarding migration.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:10.880
And you can access it
at any point of time

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:13.100
to get additional information
about our products,

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:14.900
our partners'
products, our service

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:17.240
integrator partners'
products and offerings,

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:19.835
so on and so forth.

00:06:19.835 --> 00:06:21.210
So the first phase
of the journey

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:24.300
starts with discovery,
assessment, and planning.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:27.090
And what we have done here
is that we have closely

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:32.190
partnered with a large
number of ISV vendors,

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:35.400
like Cloud Physics, Stratozone,
Risc Network, Cloudamize,

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:39.450
who have built tools
to discover environment

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:41.350
and helping in the
planning process.

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:46.740
And many of these tools are free
for customers migrating to GCP.

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:48.148
It's not free for everybody.

00:06:48.148 --> 00:06:50.190
But what we have done is
structured the agreement

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:51.630
that if you want to
use any of these tools,

00:06:51.630 --> 00:06:53.172
just reach out to
us and you can have

00:06:53.172 --> 00:06:56.190
pretty much unlimited usage
of many of these tools

00:06:56.190 --> 00:07:02.610
as you migrate to GCP for
Windows and other workloads.

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:04.680
And what these tools
does is that it kind of

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:10.470
gets you an understanding
in a very non-intrusive way,

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:13.300
a map of all the applications
running in your data center.

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:15.000
There's a couple
of sample outputs.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.520
Many of these vendors are
there in our show floor.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:18.910
You can see a demo.

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:21.680
You can also see a demo in
the infrastructure upgrade--

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:23.670
Beverly and in the show floor.

00:07:23.670 --> 00:07:25.230
But it kind of
lays out that what

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:27.070
are the critical applications?

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:30.290
What are the dependencies?

00:07:30.290 --> 00:07:33.060
If I move this workload,
what other workloads gets

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:34.930
tossed and so on and so forth?

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:38.070
And one other thing that we see
a lot of our customers doing

00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:40.200
is trying to identify those
workloads that are not

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:44.290
highly interlinked and not
highly critical to get started.

00:07:44.290 --> 00:07:47.690
And then move on to more
and more complex workloads

00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:49.050
to migrate to the Cloud.

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:50.790
Ideal examples of
workloads would

00:07:50.790 --> 00:07:54.000
include things like test
and dev environments

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:56.250
or pre-production
environments and other things

00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:59.240
to kind of try out how
this thing works out.

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:02.930
The other impressive thing
that these ISV vendors

00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:04.760
have done working in
close collaboration

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:06.920
with us is that it
actually provides you

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:10.430
very clear [INAUDIBLE] outputs
of how these tools, as you

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:12.830
migrate to the cloud, how
does this thing play out

00:08:12.830 --> 00:08:14.690
from a financial perspective?

00:08:14.690 --> 00:08:17.330
So you can do a cost
benefit analysis--

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:20.780
doing nothing
versus moving to us.

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:24.560
And while talking about the cost
benefit analysis financials,

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:28.190
one of the biggest things
that are hindering or is

00:08:28.190 --> 00:08:30.260
a concern for a lot
of customers migrating

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:33.289
Windows workload to the cloud is
the licensing component of it.

00:08:33.289 --> 00:08:35.622
And I just want to spend one
or two minutes just talking

00:08:35.622 --> 00:08:37.530
about that in a
little bit of details.

00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:41.480
The first thing to remember
is that if you are a Microsoft

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:45.050
Enterprise Agreement
customer, there

00:08:45.050 --> 00:08:49.670
are two primary tenets that
applies to license compliance.

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:53.150
Number one is that the licenses
that you bought from Microsoft

00:08:53.150 --> 00:08:55.550
using Enterprise
Agreements, they

00:08:55.550 --> 00:08:59.630
need to be used in an
infrastructure that

00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:01.230
is dedicated to you.

00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:05.090
And the next one is even when
you are using, let's say,

00:09:05.090 --> 00:09:07.940
Windows Server licenses in
infrastructure that is meant

00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:11.600
for you, you cannot move
those licenses around multiple

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:14.660
servers in any time
less than 90 days.

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:18.680
So what that means is that for
many public cloud, the way it

00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:21.920
is architected, we
create large pools

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:25.070
of physical infrastructure
and move VMs around.

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:27.560
And a lot of times the
VMs are moved around

00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:30.740
even for upgrading the
underlying physical

00:09:30.740 --> 00:09:32.920
infrastructure, even
if the certain hosts

00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:34.490
are dedicated to you.

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:36.140
So it's not Google specific.

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:41.150
For pretty much all of
the public cloud vendors,

00:09:41.150 --> 00:09:45.020
what it means is that you have
to, till now, buy something

00:09:45.020 --> 00:09:48.680
called a SPLA license for
running Windows on public cloud

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:49.580
systems.

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:52.370
And SPLA is amazing for
very specific workloads,

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:54.890
like for example, you have
a-- and a SPLA is basically

00:09:54.890 --> 00:09:57.050
Windows licenses or
other Microsoft license

00:09:57.050 --> 00:09:59.250
you can buy by the second.

00:09:59.250 --> 00:10:02.780
So it's very, very good for
very highly spiky usages

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:06.350
where you are using something
for five seconds for hundreds

00:10:06.350 --> 00:10:09.080
and hundreds of calls and
don't use it for the year.

00:10:09.080 --> 00:10:13.070
It's very advantageous for those
types of workloads to use SPLA.

00:10:13.070 --> 00:10:15.860
But on other hand,
if you have already

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:17.860
your basement full
of Windows licenses

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:20.090
or SQL server
licenses, and you don't

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:23.690
want to spend money on
re-buying those licenses--

00:10:23.690 --> 00:10:24.810
and they can be costly.

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:28.130
They can be up to 60%
of the price of a VM--

00:10:28.130 --> 00:10:30.500
it can become a
prohibitive expense.

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:34.210
So that has been,
historically, a challenge.

00:10:34.210 --> 00:10:38.020
But I'm happy to
announce that next we

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:43.400
have announced the beta of
our sole tenant BYOL offering.

00:10:43.400 --> 00:10:46.160
And this is really,
really exciting,

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:48.530
because it allows you to
bring your Enterprise License

00:10:48.530 --> 00:10:51.080
Agreement into GCP.

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:54.200
So what it basically
means is that you do not

00:10:54.200 --> 00:10:59.180
have to spend a huge amount of
dollars buying SPLA licenses.

00:10:59.180 --> 00:11:02.480
If you have Enterprise Agreement
Licenses for Windows Server,

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:06.350
SQL Server, you can
dump them on GCP,

00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:08.210
run them on the
sole tenant nodes.

00:11:08.210 --> 00:11:10.640
And not only do you get
benefit of the licensing,

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:14.690
it also has a lot of the inbuilt
reporting that in case you

00:11:14.690 --> 00:11:18.350
get audited by Microsoft, you
can leverage these reports

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:19.980
for compliance purposes.

00:11:19.980 --> 00:11:21.890
And we have seen a
huge amount of interest

00:11:21.890 --> 00:11:23.510
in the alpha and
the beta customers

00:11:23.510 --> 00:11:24.740
we have been working with.

00:11:24.740 --> 00:11:28.220
So definitely encourage you
guys to try it out and figure it

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:30.706
out-- dig into this
a little bit more.

00:11:30.706 --> 00:11:33.750
And closely associated
with licensing cost,

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:37.140
it's not necessarily
just the BYOL.

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:40.950
The Google Cloud Platform itself
has a large number of primitive

00:11:40.950 --> 00:11:44.540
that helps you optimize
licensing and infrastructure

00:11:44.540 --> 00:11:45.040
costs.

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:48.240
For example, things like per
second billing, custom VM

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:51.960
sizes, pre-emptible VMs,
right sizing, recommendations

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:54.840
so that you are not using extra
cores, which usually translate

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:56.310
to extra licensing costs.

00:11:56.310 --> 00:12:00.930
So altogether it creates a
very significant difference

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:04.480
between what you may have
spent on licensing before

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:09.010
to move to public cloud,
than doing it with us now.

00:12:09.010 --> 00:12:10.980
So definitely we
are very excited.

00:12:10.980 --> 00:12:13.600
And we expect a lot of customer
adoption and excitement

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:14.100
on that.

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:19.250
Once you have figured out the
licensing and how to migrate,

00:12:19.250 --> 00:12:23.240
the next big thing is how do
we actually do that migration.

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:26.390
And I will hand it over
to my colleague Simon

00:12:26.390 --> 00:12:29.616
to kind of walk through
a quick demo of the--

00:12:29.616 --> 00:12:31.690
SIMON ZELTSER:
Thank you, Souvik.

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:34.710
So with Velostrata, it
actually makes it very,

00:12:34.710 --> 00:12:38.630
very easy to migrate your VM
running on-premises the way

00:12:38.630 --> 00:12:40.310
to Google Cloud.

00:12:40.310 --> 00:12:43.040
When you use Velostrata,
you can basically

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:45.380
take your
applications, which are

00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:47.540
up and running with
Windows or with Linux

00:12:47.540 --> 00:12:50.780
as well, without installing
anything on the VM,

00:12:50.780 --> 00:12:53.120
without changing
your source code,

00:12:53.120 --> 00:12:58.670
basically just taking over those
VMs all the way into the cloud.

00:12:58.670 --> 00:13:02.750
Now the solution allows
you to do easy testing,

00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:04.910
easy rolling back.

00:13:04.910 --> 00:13:07.580
If you move the
VMs into the cloud,

00:13:07.580 --> 00:13:11.150
and then you decide to move
them back, you can do that too.

00:13:11.150 --> 00:13:14.180
Also, if you have
waves of machines,

00:13:14.180 --> 00:13:19.010
for example, cluster that
is multiple VMs working

00:13:19.010 --> 00:13:20.870
with each other
with dependencies,

00:13:20.870 --> 00:13:23.990
you can use the feature called
Run Book Automation which

00:13:23.990 --> 00:13:26.690
allows you to move
all those VMs together

00:13:26.690 --> 00:13:30.440
such that the dependencies
will be preserved.

00:13:30.440 --> 00:13:32.045
I'm going to show
you a small demo.

00:13:34.630 --> 00:13:36.370
All right, thank you.

00:13:36.370 --> 00:13:40.110
So what we have here is
the vSphere web client.

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:44.370
And I have VMware data
center with multiple VMs--

00:13:44.370 --> 00:13:48.170
in this case, two Windows VMs.

00:13:48.170 --> 00:13:52.710
What I am doing here in one of
those VMs is running regular

00:13:52.710 --> 00:13:54.840
legacy .NET framework
application.

00:13:54.840 --> 00:13:57.400
It is hosted on IAS.

00:13:57.400 --> 00:14:01.350
And just to show
you what that is,

00:14:01.350 --> 00:14:07.240
it's a open source tool called
Orchard for Content Management.

00:14:07.240 --> 00:14:09.780
It's currently
running on-premises.

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:13.710
And we'll try to move this
application all the way

00:14:13.710 --> 00:14:15.975
into the cloud.

00:14:18.750 --> 00:14:19.740
So I'm all ready.

00:14:19.740 --> 00:14:22.110
I have all the setup baked in.

00:14:22.110 --> 00:14:27.630
So all we need to do is go
into this Velostrata Operations

00:14:27.630 --> 00:14:32.280
tab, which you need to install
as part of your vSphere web

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:33.640
client.

00:14:33.640 --> 00:14:36.375
And then we have this
option of migrate.

00:14:40.220 --> 00:14:42.890
What it does it
opens up a wizard,

00:14:42.890 --> 00:14:46.040
which we need to
fill up to say how

00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:50.570
is this VM going to be
named once it's ported over

00:14:50.570 --> 00:14:52.820
to Google Cloud.

00:14:52.820 --> 00:14:55.640
And then what is
the VM configuration

00:14:55.640 --> 00:14:59.720
in terms of CPU and RAM?

00:14:59.720 --> 00:15:05.590
This storage policy, how are we
going to handle all the writes?

00:15:05.590 --> 00:15:08.090
And then the networking,
in this case,

00:15:08.090 --> 00:15:11.140
I'm going to create
an ephemeral IP

00:15:11.140 --> 00:15:15.400
address so that we can try and
access this VM on Google Cloud

00:15:15.400 --> 00:15:17.830
later on.

00:15:17.830 --> 00:15:23.350
And then I'm basically
going to run this operation.

00:15:23.350 --> 00:15:27.950
Now this operation can take
about 10 minutes to complete.

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:30.820
But what we will
see eventually is

00:15:30.820 --> 00:15:33.760
I already have one of
those machines moved,

00:15:33.760 --> 00:15:39.530
I moved the win-1 when we just
started this presentation.

00:15:39.530 --> 00:15:43.850
So I can show you that
this VM one is actually

00:15:43.850 --> 00:15:48.260
accessible through the external
IP address on Google Cloud.

00:15:48.260 --> 00:15:49.130
This is the machine.

00:15:52.360 --> 00:15:54.160
And all the effort
that I had to do

00:15:54.160 --> 00:15:58.600
is just install the VMware
extension for Velostrata,

00:15:58.600 --> 00:16:02.240
and also install the server
side part of Velostrata

00:16:02.240 --> 00:16:05.290
on my project at Google Cloud.

00:16:05.290 --> 00:16:08.590
And then create the
VPN interconnect

00:16:08.590 --> 00:16:11.290
between the on-premises
and the cloud.

00:16:11.290 --> 00:16:13.690
I did all the setup
ahead of time,

00:16:13.690 --> 00:16:17.380
but basically once
you get the setup up

00:16:17.380 --> 00:16:19.990
and running, and we have
everything documented

00:16:19.990 --> 00:16:25.390
on cloud.google.com, you can
migrate your VMs for free

00:16:25.390 --> 00:16:27.770
without changing anything
in the source code,

00:16:27.770 --> 00:16:31.420
or even without the VM being
aware of the fact that it's

00:16:31.420 --> 00:16:32.350
moving.

00:16:32.350 --> 00:16:36.130
Moreover, there is no downtime
that's caused by the fact

00:16:36.130 --> 00:16:37.542
that you migrate those VMs.

00:16:37.542 --> 00:16:39.250
So it's really powerful
solution that you

00:16:39.250 --> 00:16:43.470
can use even today if you
want to migrate your Windows

00:16:43.470 --> 00:16:46.330
VMs onto a Google Cloud.

00:16:46.330 --> 00:16:48.395
So let's switch back to Souvik.

00:16:48.395 --> 00:16:49.767
SOUVIK CHOUDHURY: Thanks Simon.

00:16:49.767 --> 00:16:50.850
And that's pretty awesome.

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:52.900
And by the way,
just to highlight,

00:16:52.900 --> 00:16:55.660
you can see the GCP
is automatically

00:16:55.660 --> 00:16:59.240
recommending how you can
optimize VM sizes to save

00:16:59.240 --> 00:17:00.940
certain amount of dollars.

00:17:00.940 --> 00:17:02.890
And all of those
numbers are probably

00:17:02.890 --> 00:17:05.020
going to be double,
because it's going

00:17:05.020 --> 00:17:08.170
to also save Windows
licensing costs as you

00:17:08.170 --> 00:17:09.130
go through this phase.

00:17:09.130 --> 00:17:12.023
Just wanted to highlight this.

00:17:12.023 --> 00:17:13.440
Switch back to the
slides, please.

00:17:19.660 --> 00:17:21.160
So the next phase
of the journey,

00:17:21.160 --> 00:17:24.700
now that we have landed
your existing VMs on GCP,

00:17:24.700 --> 00:17:27.130
is how do we operate and manage
this environment on a day

00:17:27.130 --> 00:17:28.930
to day basis?

00:17:28.930 --> 00:17:31.270
And over here, some of
the key questions we hear

00:17:31.270 --> 00:17:34.720
is how do I deploy new
VMs or apps on GCP?

00:17:34.720 --> 00:17:35.470
Is it secure?

00:17:35.470 --> 00:17:36.730
How is the performance?

00:17:36.730 --> 00:17:38.140
How do I manage my application?

00:17:38.140 --> 00:17:40.600
Do I have to do all the stuff
that I was doing in the data

00:17:40.600 --> 00:17:44.590
center on GCP, or there's better
way of achieving the same thing

00:17:44.590 --> 00:17:47.090
and so on and so forth?

00:17:47.090 --> 00:17:49.570
And a couple of points
in that direction.

00:17:49.570 --> 00:17:52.390
So once you have migrated,
you may have needs

00:17:52.390 --> 00:17:55.750
for new VMs and new
applications to be spun up.

00:17:55.750 --> 00:17:57.670
And what you will
see at GCP is that we

00:17:57.670 --> 00:18:02.680
have a very, very large library
of pre-engineered, pretested,

00:18:02.680 --> 00:18:06.670
pre-optimized Google
Windows images

00:18:06.670 --> 00:18:08.380
that you can spin
up whenever you

00:18:08.380 --> 00:18:12.430
want with integrated licensing
and all these management tied

00:18:12.430 --> 00:18:14.110
into GCP.

00:18:14.110 --> 00:18:17.290
In case you can't
find exactly what

00:18:17.290 --> 00:18:20.530
application you are looking
for, what you will also find

00:18:20.530 --> 00:18:27.490
is a large number of prepackaged
applications as images that are

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:29.230
available in our marketplace.

00:18:29.230 --> 00:18:31.330
And the billing, the
licensing, the usage

00:18:31.330 --> 00:18:33.920
are all tied to your
overall GCP account.

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:37.450
So it's really, really easy
for you to spin up applications

00:18:37.450 --> 00:18:40.780
that you might want
from ISV vendors,

00:18:40.780 --> 00:18:44.370
as you can see over here.

00:18:44.370 --> 00:18:48.150
I know many of you have much
more strict and stringent

00:18:48.150 --> 00:18:49.800
security requirements.

00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:51.330
I just wanted to
highlight that all

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:53.760
of these VMs from us
or from a marketplace

00:18:53.760 --> 00:18:56.430
can actually be done on
our Shielded VMs, which

00:18:56.430 --> 00:19:00.480
makes them even more secure to
run your workloads for Windows

00:19:00.480 --> 00:19:01.440
on GCP.

00:19:01.440 --> 00:19:04.290
And this is in addition to
all these other great security

00:19:04.290 --> 00:19:08.627
features we have
already tied into GCP.

00:19:08.627 --> 00:19:11.210
The other thing that I want to
also [INAUDIBLE] highlight over

00:19:11.210 --> 00:19:16.190
here is our huge differentiator
from an operational lifetime

00:19:16.190 --> 00:19:20.210
perspective, which
is live migration.

00:19:20.210 --> 00:19:25.280
So whenever we have to make
infrastructure firmware updates

00:19:25.280 --> 00:19:30.020
to our underlying
public cloud platform,

00:19:30.020 --> 00:19:31.940
using live migration
technology we

00:19:31.940 --> 00:19:35.270
make sure that none of
your workloads get impacted

00:19:35.270 --> 00:19:37.382
and there is
continued operation.

00:19:37.382 --> 00:19:38.840
So if you're worried
about hey, I'm

00:19:38.840 --> 00:19:43.280
going to start running my
Windows applications here,

00:19:43.280 --> 00:19:44.317
is it reliable?

00:19:44.317 --> 00:19:45.650
Are we going to suffer downtime?

00:19:45.650 --> 00:19:47.733
The answer is that you can
be completely confident

00:19:47.733 --> 00:19:48.937
that we've got you covered.

00:19:48.937 --> 00:19:51.020
We have live migration and
many other technologies

00:19:51.020 --> 00:19:54.740
too, which applies for Windows
as well as any other workloads

00:19:54.740 --> 00:19:57.960
to provide higher uptime.

00:19:57.960 --> 00:20:00.420
So I'm also pleased
to announce a number

00:20:00.420 --> 00:20:03.390
of new additional
services as promised.

00:20:03.390 --> 00:20:06.390
We are working on the GCP
patch and conflict management.

00:20:06.390 --> 00:20:08.677
The purpose of this
is to make sure

00:20:08.677 --> 00:20:11.010
that when you are running all
these Windows VMs, as well

00:20:11.010 --> 00:20:13.140
as Linux and other
VMs, you do not

00:20:13.140 --> 00:20:15.720
have to manually keep on
patching and extending your

00:20:15.720 --> 00:20:17.700
on prem applications to GCP.

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:21.060
This is a very simple click
through console integrated

00:20:21.060 --> 00:20:24.930
into the GCP console, which
allows you to understand how

00:20:24.930 --> 00:20:26.670
many of your VMs
that are running

00:20:26.670 --> 00:20:30.210
are at a certain batch
level [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:30.210 --> 00:20:31.710
level, based on risk practices.

00:20:31.710 --> 00:20:33.960
And if they are
not at par, you can

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:37.950
set policies to either upgrade
them or take additional steps.

00:20:37.950 --> 00:20:42.750
You can also drive policy
saying that all the VMs running

00:20:42.750 --> 00:20:46.080
in this projects needs to
have these configurations,

00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:49.140
because that's what you
mandate, that's what is secure,

00:20:49.140 --> 00:20:52.110
and that's how you kind
of enforce standardization

00:20:52.110 --> 00:20:54.030
across your fleet of VMs.

00:20:54.030 --> 00:20:58.710
If we detect any of these VMs do
not comply with these policies,

00:20:58.710 --> 00:21:01.980
you can trigger actions,
including highlighting them

00:21:01.980 --> 00:21:04.890
in a report and other things,
so that it makes it much, much

00:21:04.890 --> 00:21:09.420
easier for you to manage these
large fleets of VMs right

00:21:09.420 --> 00:21:12.270
from the console without
having to use third party tools

00:21:12.270 --> 00:21:14.820
or extending on prem tools.

00:21:14.820 --> 00:21:18.413
And we are looking for alpha
partners to test this out.

00:21:18.413 --> 00:21:19.830
We expect to get
this out sometime

00:21:19.830 --> 00:21:21.000
before the end of the year.

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:23.400
And my colleague, Ravi, here--

00:21:23.400 --> 00:21:29.160
just feel free to stop by and
ask him for additional details.

00:21:29.160 --> 00:21:33.480
We're also very, very excited
to announce our managed SQL

00:21:33.480 --> 00:21:36.150
offering on GCP.

00:21:36.150 --> 00:21:40.230
So we already had a large number
of standardized SQL images

00:21:40.230 --> 00:21:42.903
that are optimized for
GCP you could spin up.

00:21:42.903 --> 00:21:44.820
But one of the things
that we heard repeatedly

00:21:44.820 --> 00:21:48.900
from our customers is, hey,
I have a 1,000, 2,000, 3,000

00:21:48.900 --> 00:21:49.980
instances of SQL.

00:21:49.980 --> 00:21:52.710
I really don't want to manage
that once they're on GCP.

00:21:52.710 --> 00:21:54.300
You guys should be
doing more of that

00:21:54.300 --> 00:21:56.670
so that I can focus
more of my efforts

00:21:56.670 --> 00:22:00.490
on higher order business
value generating problems.

00:22:00.490 --> 00:22:02.140
And that's what we have.

00:22:02.140 --> 00:22:05.880
So you can actually
spin up these SQL VMs

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:08.130
and we will manage them
for you under an SLA.

00:22:08.130 --> 00:22:12.090
It's fully compliant to
Microsoft Work environments

00:22:12.090 --> 00:22:13.470
and takes another load off you.

00:22:16.170 --> 00:22:23.190
Very similar to SQL, AD is
another integral part of GCP.

00:22:23.190 --> 00:22:25.170
And we already had AD service.

00:22:25.170 --> 00:22:28.260
But we are happy to
[INAUDIBLE] the beta or coming

00:22:28.260 --> 00:22:31.470
soon for the beta for
the Managed AD service.

00:22:31.470 --> 00:22:33.687
And I will hand it over
to my partner Simon

00:22:33.687 --> 00:22:36.270
again, to kind of walk through
that and show you a quick demo.

00:22:36.270 --> 00:22:38.670
SIMON ZELTSER: Thank you Souvik.

00:22:38.670 --> 00:22:41.940
So with Managed Service for
Microsoft Active Directory,

00:22:41.940 --> 00:22:45.030
what you get is Microsoft
Active Directory,

00:22:45.030 --> 00:22:48.180
but hardened and
managed by Google.

00:22:48.180 --> 00:22:52.230
You get the system
configuration, patching,

00:22:52.230 --> 00:22:54.383
replication, all
of those hassles

00:22:54.383 --> 00:22:56.550
that you typically need to
deal with when you manage

00:22:56.550 --> 00:23:00.330
Active Directory yourself, you
get that all offset and taken

00:23:00.330 --> 00:23:04.550
care of by a Google Operations
team and our service

00:23:04.550 --> 00:23:07.770
that we just announced recently.

00:23:07.770 --> 00:23:11.610
And the beta of Microsoft Active
Directory Managed by Google

00:23:11.610 --> 00:23:14.650
is going to be announced
very, very soon.

00:23:14.650 --> 00:23:15.810
So look out for that.

00:23:15.810 --> 00:23:20.220
If you want to join the alpha
and express more interest,

00:23:20.220 --> 00:23:26.460
there is a link on the slides
that you can join and get

00:23:26.460 --> 00:23:28.260
some more details.

00:23:28.260 --> 00:23:31.290
You can extend your
existing Active Directory

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:33.840
from on-premises all
the way to Google Cloud,

00:23:33.840 --> 00:23:37.710
and manage their identities
in a unified way using

00:23:37.710 --> 00:23:39.240
this offering.

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:41.460
I want to show you a
small demo of that.

00:23:44.010 --> 00:23:47.700
What you see here is
our cloud console.

00:23:47.700 --> 00:23:51.360
And on the Security tab
there is a new option

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:53.670
called Managed Microsoft AD.

00:23:53.670 --> 00:23:57.120
This is where we can create your
own Microsoft Active Directory

00:23:57.120 --> 00:23:58.540
Domain.

00:23:58.540 --> 00:24:01.890
So all I need to do just going
into the Create AD Domain

00:24:01.890 --> 00:24:10.080
and add some name, networking
settings, and which region I

00:24:10.080 --> 00:24:14.320
want to deploy this
Active Directory setup.

00:24:14.320 --> 00:24:18.010
And then I can also
name the admin user.

00:24:18.010 --> 00:24:20.040
This admin user
is what you get so

00:24:20.040 --> 00:24:23.580
that you can manage all your
identities using that user.

00:24:23.580 --> 00:24:28.650
You can also delegate starting
from this user to other users

00:24:28.650 --> 00:24:29.910
that you might create.

00:24:29.910 --> 00:24:32.640
Basically you can use the
same familiar Active Directory

00:24:32.640 --> 00:24:35.790
tools like Active Directory
Users and Computers

00:24:35.790 --> 00:24:38.680
to manage all your
Active Directory needs.

00:24:38.680 --> 00:24:42.880
But this user is going to be
your gateway into the Managed

00:24:42.880 --> 00:24:44.100
Active Directory.

00:24:44.100 --> 00:24:48.000
And you will get full control
of all the user's computers

00:24:48.000 --> 00:24:51.960
and other Active Directory
objects that you might create.

00:24:51.960 --> 00:24:55.445
Once I hit Create here, it's
going to take about 40 minutes

00:24:55.445 --> 00:24:55.945
to create.

00:24:55.945 --> 00:24:59.310
So I already have a
few domains out there.

00:24:59.310 --> 00:25:01.590
Once I create this
domain, you can be sure

00:25:01.590 --> 00:25:03.600
that it's all managed by Google.

00:25:03.600 --> 00:25:06.870
There are multiple domain
controller instances

00:25:06.870 --> 00:25:10.740
that we spin up so that
it's good for redundancy

00:25:10.740 --> 00:25:15.270
and for overall health of
your Active Directory setup.

00:25:18.882 --> 00:25:20.340
SOUVIK CHOUDHURY:
Thank you, Simon.

