really closely with Microsoft
on Windows Server Container

00:27:08.550 --> 00:27:09.950
Support in GKE.

00:27:09.950 --> 00:27:11.630
We are really
excited, because this

00:27:11.630 --> 00:27:15.380
allows you to provide
a seamless operating

00:27:15.380 --> 00:27:18.770
experience across Linux and
Windows containers on GCP

00:27:18.770 --> 00:27:20.660
and really optimize
your operations.

00:27:20.660 --> 00:27:24.680
And my colleague,
[INAUDIBLE] here,

00:27:24.680 --> 00:27:29.670
will be around to answer any
questions after the session.

00:27:29.670 --> 00:27:32.522
So now that we have talked
about all these instruments

00:27:32.522 --> 00:27:33.980
that we have at
our disposal, let's

00:27:33.980 --> 00:27:37.220
talk about how you
kind of use all of them

00:27:37.220 --> 00:27:39.630
to modernize your applications.

00:27:39.630 --> 00:27:41.080
So the first and
the easiest case

00:27:41.080 --> 00:27:44.750
is probably new or existing
apps that can be rewritten

00:27:44.750 --> 00:27:48.090
or you want to rewrite for GCP.

00:27:48.090 --> 00:27:51.390
And over here you
have GCP, .NET, APIs,

00:27:51.390 --> 00:27:53.550
various types of
tools we talked about,

00:27:53.550 --> 00:27:55.290
like the VisualStudio
integration

00:27:55.290 --> 00:27:59.640
and the Stackdriver, to rewrite
those applications and run that

00:27:59.640 --> 00:28:03.003
on App Engine, which is
entirely cloud native.

00:28:03.003 --> 00:28:04.170
You don't have to manage it.

00:28:04.170 --> 00:28:05.860
You don't have to
think about it.

00:28:05.860 --> 00:28:07.350
It kind of
[INAUDIBLE] magically.

00:28:07.350 --> 00:28:08.965
But we also know
that that's probably

00:28:08.965 --> 00:28:11.340
going to be a small percentage
of the overall application

00:28:11.340 --> 00:28:12.480
that you manage.

00:28:12.480 --> 00:28:14.108
So what do you do with them?

00:28:14.108 --> 00:28:16.650
So there are going to be some
applications that you are going

00:28:16.650 --> 00:28:21.360
to be running on .NET that
can be converted to .NET Core

00:28:21.360 --> 00:28:22.410
applications.

00:28:22.410 --> 00:28:29.490
And we understand that .NET
Core is not really as expansive

00:28:29.490 --> 00:28:30.890
as to write all applications.

00:28:30.890 --> 00:28:33.530
But a certain set of
applications you can migrate.

00:28:33.530 --> 00:28:36.150
And what you can do is
that as you re-architect

00:28:36.150 --> 00:28:40.440
your applications to .NET Core,
you can actually run .NET Core

00:28:40.440 --> 00:28:42.880
on a Linux on a GKE server.

00:28:42.880 --> 00:28:47.370
So what happens is your overall
operating platform is now much

00:28:47.370 --> 00:28:50.680
more open, because .NET
Core can learn on Linux,

00:28:50.680 --> 00:28:53.575
and it's moving more and
more towards open source.

00:28:53.575 --> 00:28:55.950
Now there are going to be a
set of applications where you

00:28:55.950 --> 00:28:57.230
can't convert to .NET.

00:28:57.230 --> 00:29:00.140
This may be traditional
.NET applications,

00:29:00.140 --> 00:29:02.310
maybe even Windows
32 applications.

00:29:02.310 --> 00:29:09.750
What you can do there is
convert them to GKE containers,

00:29:09.750 --> 00:29:13.590
and run them on a manage
GKE service on Windows.

00:29:13.590 --> 00:29:19.080
So what happens is that
it's not completely open.

00:29:19.080 --> 00:29:20.310
It's not completely scalable.

00:29:20.310 --> 00:29:22.055
You still have a
Windows footprint there,

00:29:22.055 --> 00:29:23.430
but you have
actually taken a lot

00:29:23.430 --> 00:29:26.280
of the pain out of the customer
environment of the platform

00:29:26.280 --> 00:29:29.830
by making it part of the
overall GKE solution.

00:29:29.830 --> 00:29:34.260
And finally, there are going
to be Windows applications that

00:29:34.260 --> 00:29:35.970
can't even be containerized.

00:29:35.970 --> 00:29:39.450
Maybe they're really old or have
some special quirks that stops

00:29:39.450 --> 00:29:40.950
it from being containerized.

00:29:40.950 --> 00:29:46.410
And for those applications,
you can still move them to GCP,

00:29:46.410 --> 00:29:47.460
like we talked about.

00:29:47.460 --> 00:29:49.620
And you still get
all the benefits

00:29:49.620 --> 00:29:57.930
of managed services, licensing,
things like managed instance

00:29:57.930 --> 00:30:00.990
groups that makes it easier for
you to scale up and scale down

00:30:00.990 --> 00:30:03.580
the resource usage,
and so on and so forth.

00:30:03.580 --> 00:30:06.990
And hopefully, you know,
initially when you start,

00:30:06.990 --> 00:30:08.670
most of the application
that you have

00:30:08.670 --> 00:30:11.050
are going to be the
bottom part of the slide.

00:30:11.050 --> 00:30:14.400
But as time goes by, you kind
of start moving your apps

00:30:14.400 --> 00:30:16.445
more and more up the stack.

00:30:16.445 --> 00:30:17.820
And at some point
of time, you're

00:30:17.820 --> 00:30:22.360
in a much more free and open
world than you are right now.

00:30:22.360 --> 00:30:24.550
So that's how this
thing hangs together.

00:30:24.550 --> 00:30:28.980
And I would hand over to my
colleague for this final time

00:30:28.980 --> 00:30:32.980
to kind of show a demo of how
all these things work together.

00:30:32.980 --> 00:30:34.910
SIMON ZELTSER:
Thank you, Souvik.

00:30:34.910 --> 00:30:36.560
So can I switch back please?

00:30:39.940 --> 00:30:40.620
All right.

00:30:40.620 --> 00:30:41.920
Thank you.

00:30:41.920 --> 00:30:45.090
So we are going to see a demo
how some of the tools that

00:30:45.090 --> 00:30:48.540
Souvik mentioned earlier for
modernization are playing

00:30:48.540 --> 00:30:53.970
together when you develop your
.NET or .NET Core application.

00:30:53.970 --> 00:30:55.470
In order to understand
the demo, we

00:30:55.470 --> 00:30:56.887
thought that it's
going to be very

00:30:56.887 --> 00:30:58.860
useful to see the
storyboard first,

00:30:58.860 --> 00:31:01.920
and then we actually
will go through the demo.

00:31:01.920 --> 00:31:03.720
So at first, we'll
see application,

00:31:03.720 --> 00:31:07.530
which is quite typical once
you start modernizing your .NET

00:31:07.530 --> 00:31:09.480
framework to .NET Core.

00:31:09.480 --> 00:31:12.690
And this is the application
with three layers.

00:31:12.690 --> 00:31:16.110
You have front end talking to a
back end talking to a database.

00:31:16.110 --> 00:31:20.130
All those are microservices
that run on Kubernetes

00:31:20.130 --> 00:31:23.430
cluster on a managed GKE.

00:31:23.430 --> 00:31:24.720
And there is a problem there.

00:31:27.230 --> 00:31:30.080
In order to understand
and diagnose the problem,

00:31:30.080 --> 00:31:32.930
we'll use tools such as
Stackdriver Debugger with

00:31:32.930 --> 00:31:36.370
Logpoint Support for .NET Core.

00:31:36.370 --> 00:31:39.300
And we'll try to
spot the problem.

00:31:39.300 --> 00:31:43.710
We'll then make an update into
the code using the tool that we

00:31:43.710 --> 00:31:47.280
are happy to announce today
called Cloud Code for Visual

00:31:47.280 --> 00:31:51.540
Studio Code with
.NET Core Support.

00:31:51.540 --> 00:31:55.070
And then after the deployment
using that tool is complete,

00:31:55.070 --> 00:31:59.580
we'll see that the application
is all up and running again

00:31:59.580 --> 00:32:01.140
on Google Cloud.

00:32:01.140 --> 00:32:05.760
So now let's please
switch back to my laptop.

00:32:05.760 --> 00:32:08.850
What you see here is the
Guestbook application

00:32:08.850 --> 00:32:10.830
that I just mentioned earlier.

00:32:10.830 --> 00:32:14.720
So you can type some text.

00:32:14.720 --> 00:32:20.320
And then you should be
able to post this text

00:32:20.320 --> 00:32:23.140
into the Guestbook, which
would store it eventually

00:32:23.140 --> 00:32:24.940
in some database.

00:32:24.940 --> 00:32:30.800
But it takes some time, and
apparently doesn't work.

00:32:30.800 --> 00:32:33.890
So probably it happened
to some of you in the past

00:32:33.890 --> 00:32:35.960
that it works on my
machine, but when

00:32:35.960 --> 00:32:37.520
I move it to a
different environment,

00:32:37.520 --> 00:32:39.090
it stops working
for some reason.

00:32:39.090 --> 00:32:41.360
So the problem might
be not necessarily

00:32:41.360 --> 00:32:45.200
relevant to the code,
but more so relevant

00:32:45.200 --> 00:32:48.180
to the changes between
different environments.

00:32:48.180 --> 00:32:52.220
And so we want to understand
what the problem is.

00:32:52.220 --> 00:32:56.180
So I open the tool called
Stackdriver Debugger.

00:32:56.180 --> 00:32:58.150
Stackdriver Debugger
is a tool that

00:32:58.150 --> 00:33:02.350
allows you to troubleshoot
applications live in production

00:33:02.350 --> 00:33:04.660
doing what we call
snapshot debugging,

00:33:04.660 --> 00:33:06.790
as well as attaching logpoints.

00:33:06.790 --> 00:33:09.550
I'll talk more about the
logpoints experience.

00:33:09.550 --> 00:33:12.490
With logpoints, you can add
additional logging statement

00:33:12.490 --> 00:33:16.600
in already running application
without changing its source

00:33:16.600 --> 00:33:19.210
code, but with adding
more context for you

00:33:19.210 --> 00:33:20.860
to troubleshoot the problem.

00:33:20.860 --> 00:33:26.860
What we'll do here is adding
a statement on line 46--

00:33:26.860 --> 00:33:28.720
this is the code
of the application

00:33:28.720 --> 00:33:30.350
that you saw earlier--

00:33:30.350 --> 00:33:33.160
I am adding a logpoint.

00:33:33.160 --> 00:33:36.250
I can make it conditional,
but I'll, just for simplicity,

00:33:36.250 --> 00:33:40.500
will add it with an
always true statement.

00:33:40.500 --> 00:33:49.470
And I am going to write
backendAddr equals--

00:33:49.470 --> 00:33:53.290
basically, there is a
variable on line 46.

00:33:53.290 --> 00:33:55.980
It's sending a
request to a back end,

00:33:55.980 --> 00:33:58.630
but I don't know what
back end address is that.

00:33:58.630 --> 00:34:03.570
So for that, I'll try to
print that value into the log.

00:34:10.239 --> 00:34:14.949
So once we do that, we
basically added a logpoint

00:34:14.949 --> 00:34:16.190
without changing a code.

00:34:16.190 --> 00:34:19.060
The way it works
is that we, which

00:34:19.060 --> 00:34:22.489
is Stackdriver in this
case, react to callback.

00:34:22.489 --> 00:34:25.030
So once the code
password we added

00:34:25.030 --> 00:34:27.310
the logpoint is
being hit, there will

00:34:27.310 --> 00:34:30.730
be a callback that will send
the log message that we just

00:34:30.730 --> 00:34:33.489
added into Stackdriver
Logger so that you will

00:34:33.489 --> 00:34:36.790
be able to see this additional
log statement that wasn't there

00:34:36.790 --> 00:34:39.070
before when you actually
deployed the application

00:34:39.070 --> 00:34:40.580
in the first place.

00:34:40.580 --> 00:34:43.389
This allows you to
troubleshoot applications

00:34:43.389 --> 00:34:47.030
without the need to redeploy
by adding more log statements.

00:34:47.030 --> 00:34:50.050
It could be very useful,
because sometimes deployment

00:34:50.050 --> 00:34:51.940
could be a stressful operation.

00:34:51.940 --> 00:34:55.270
Also with some of the safe
deployment practices out there,

00:34:55.270 --> 00:34:58.660
it might take some time to
actually roll out the changes

00:34:58.660 --> 00:35:00.560
that you're making
into your application.

00:35:00.560 --> 00:35:02.960
So this could become very handy.

00:35:02.960 --> 00:35:06.380
So let's try to execute
this application again.

00:35:09.880 --> 00:35:12.280
And so by refreshing
this page, I'm

00:35:12.280 --> 00:35:15.460
actually trying to
execute the same code path

00:35:15.460 --> 00:35:18.740
where we added the logpoint.

00:35:18.740 --> 00:35:23.070
And so now if we
refresh, we should

00:35:23.070 --> 00:35:25.410
see that it actually
added this log statement,

00:35:25.410 --> 00:35:28.580
backendAddr equals.

00:35:28.580 --> 00:35:30.600
And this is the actual
value of the variable,

00:35:30.600 --> 00:35:33.420
which wasn't known before.

00:35:33.420 --> 00:35:37.190
So you can see here that
there is a typo in back end.

00:35:37.190 --> 00:35:40.430
And this is likely why the
application is not working.

00:35:40.430 --> 00:35:42.620
Now I want to deploy
a change which

00:35:42.620 --> 00:35:44.400
will fix this application.

00:35:44.400 --> 00:35:47.510
So for that I will use
a tool called Cloud Code

00:35:47.510 --> 00:35:49.340
for Visual Studio Code.

00:35:49.340 --> 00:35:52.480
This is a new extension that we
are pleased to announce today.

00:35:52.480 --> 00:35:57.020
It supports four programming
languages out of the box.

00:35:57.020 --> 00:36:01.280
And .NET Core is the fifth
language that we add to that.

00:36:01.280 --> 00:36:05.090
With this extension, you can
browse your Kubernetes cluster

00:36:05.090 --> 00:36:09.860
objects, see what ports and
deployments and other objects

00:36:09.860 --> 00:36:12.200
you have, you can
connect to specific ports

00:36:12.200 --> 00:36:13.520
for troubleshooting.

00:36:13.520 --> 00:36:16.880
You can get more details about
what's currently deployed

00:36:16.880 --> 00:36:21.890
and where without memorizing all
the QCTL commands for managing

00:36:21.890 --> 00:36:23.740
and working with
Kubernetes clusters.

00:36:23.740 --> 00:36:27.290
So it really streamlines
the inner and outer loop

00:36:27.290 --> 00:36:30.360
of your development experience.

00:36:30.360 --> 00:36:35.960
So let's make a change here,
and change buckend into backend.

00:36:35.960 --> 00:36:37.910
And then I want to deploy it.

00:36:37.910 --> 00:36:39.860
In order to deploy,
all I need to do

00:36:39.860 --> 00:36:45.020
is starting a new command,
which is Cloud Code Deploy.

00:36:45.020 --> 00:36:47.660
What this will
do, it will ask me

00:36:47.660 --> 00:36:50.100
what profile do I want to use.

00:36:50.100 --> 00:36:52.550
So this extension supports
multiple profiles.

00:36:52.550 --> 00:36:55.190
And you can think about profiles
as different environments

00:36:55.190 --> 00:36:57.300
that you might have
in your application.

00:36:57.300 --> 00:37:01.100
So maybe I want to deploy
to my local machine

00:37:01.100 --> 00:37:03.620
and see if it works,
but I can also

00:37:03.620 --> 00:37:05.210
choose a different environment.

00:37:05.210 --> 00:37:09.800
In my case, I choose to actually
deploy it into Google Cloud.

00:37:09.800 --> 00:37:12.320
And what happens
now as I speak is

00:37:12.320 --> 00:37:16.070
that the application is
packaging all my code,

00:37:16.070 --> 00:37:18.530
pushing it into GCS bucket.

00:37:18.530 --> 00:37:21.680
And then we are going
to invoke a tool

00:37:21.680 --> 00:37:23.450
called Cloud Build,
which will try

00:37:23.450 --> 00:37:26.960
to build all of the
code of my application,

00:37:26.960 --> 00:37:30.470
create a container image,
and then push that container

00:37:30.470 --> 00:37:33.470
image into my cluster.

00:37:33.470 --> 00:37:36.440
All of that is going to happen
completely automatically

00:37:36.440 --> 00:37:41.630
without you needing to deal
with all of the small pieces

00:37:41.630 --> 00:37:42.710
of how do I build?

00:37:42.710 --> 00:37:44.000
How do I debug?

00:37:44.000 --> 00:37:45.230
And how do I deploy?

00:37:45.230 --> 00:37:48.320
It's all taken care
of by this tool.

00:37:48.320 --> 00:37:52.070
Now the deployment by itself
can take a few minutes here,

00:37:52.070 --> 00:37:55.850
so we'll just let it run.

00:37:55.850 --> 00:37:58.250
But what I wanted
to show is basically

00:37:58.250 --> 00:38:02.390
how handy this tool can
become in your inner loop

00:38:02.390 --> 00:38:04.340
when you try to develop
your application

00:38:04.340 --> 00:38:08.030
and repeatedly debug
and deploy them all

00:38:08.030 --> 00:38:10.040
the way into the cloud.

00:38:10.040 --> 00:38:14.010
So to just sum up
what we saw today,

00:38:14.010 --> 00:38:16.820
we actually can develop
applications using .NET

00:38:16.820 --> 00:38:20.240
framework, move them all
the way to Google Cloud with

00:38:20.240 --> 00:38:21.560
Velostrata.

00:38:21.560 --> 00:38:24.170
And then if we
want to modernize,

00:38:24.170 --> 00:38:28.490
we can move to .NET Core, use
any of Google Cloud's public

00:38:28.490 --> 00:38:30.680
APIs using .NET
client libraries,

00:38:30.680 --> 00:38:34.250
and then use tools such as
Stackdriver Debugger with

00:38:34.250 --> 00:38:39.350
Logpoint Support and Visual
Studio Code Extension Called

00:38:39.350 --> 00:38:43.220
Cloud Code with .NET Core
support to be able to build,

00:38:43.220 --> 00:38:46.820
debug, and deploy your
modernized application

00:38:46.820 --> 00:38:49.500
on Google Cloud.

00:38:49.500 --> 00:38:52.520
OK, so that's all I have.

00:38:52.520 --> 00:38:55.115
And I'd like to hand
over back to Souvik.

00:38:55.115 --> 00:38:57.042
